A||||12||Ed||Sunroof or new roof||||1||
Z||000000||Ed||10-11-2000||02:44 AM||||I'm looking at putting a sunroof on a car which currently doesn't have one.  What's the best way to put one in?<P>   1. Get OEM sunroof parts somewhere<P>   2. Get an aftermarket sunroof<P>   3. Take the whole roof off a sunroof equipped junker <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||862M4inCA||10-11-2000||04:36 AM||||Well, it's my understanding that removing the stock roof is not that big of a job, and is supposed to be accomplished without removing the windshield. I would suggest this route since you know that the stock sunroof is designed for the car. I have heard that aftermarket jobs tend to leak and have sealing problems, as well as tend not to fit into the existing hole (in the frame) for the stock sunroof. You will want to be careful when removing the donor roof to avoid using too much force to remove it as it could break the front pillars. I've been told they use some kind of mastic adheasive to hold it around the windshield, but I've never removed one and have no idea if this is the case. I DO know however that there are, I believe, 8 nuts under the headliner that also secure the roof that must be removed, as well as four along the lower front section of the roof, under the windshield. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mbramble||10-11-2000||07:27 AM||||Here is a link for instructions on removing a car roof.  <A HREF="http://www.cyberverse.com/~cyco/fieroparts/tips/sunroof.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cyberverse.com/~cyco/fieroparts/tips/sunroof.htm</A> <P>I just did this about two weeks ago.  The instructions are very good.  You must remove - the headliner to get at 6 bolts and 2 nuts; the windshield wipers so you can remove that black trim piece at the bottom of the windshield to allow you to remove some screws that hold the roof piece in place; and the seal around the windshield.  <P>Be very careful, especially with the portions that come down alongside the windshield -- they are narrow and pretty fragile.  The 'mastic' that holds the front of the top down -- all around the windshield -- is tough stuff!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||wkayl||10-11-2000||09:40 AM||||I removed one from a salvage yard for my car.  You use the piano wire around the windshield to cut the roof loose from the car.  There are 2 bolts near the front of the top behind the windshielt that are a little hard to loosen the adhesive from, but now that you know about them it will be easier than when I did it and didn't know about them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||10-11-2000||10:54 AM||||Swap roofs. I have done it to 2 cars and it's not difficult as long as you take your time and be very careful. Takes about 4 hours on average. The link that mbramble provided is how I have done it. The instructions are very well written. The only trouble you might have is if one of the cars has had it's windsheild replace by someone who didn't know what he was doing. (had a car where the windsheild was installed and they filled the gap of the roof pillars with the hard sealant.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Took almost a day to get it off without breaking it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> ) <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||10-11-2000||11:47 AM||||That happened to me too.  Someone used clear liquid nails silicone or something on mine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>I'd take the roof panel, headliner, and most of what you need from a another car.  Not sure if you can buy new seals or from a dealer, I would if they are still available.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">          <BR> <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||pollock||10-11-2000||02:00 PM||||The tough stuff that Oreif ran into was urethane.  If you run into the same sh#t, find a different donor car.  The sealant holding the A-pillar parts of the roof panel is hard enough to cut through.  <P>I just had to cut mine off AGAIN as part of my ongoing T-Top install.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ed||10-11-2000||04:13 PM||||I still can't believe what the Internet is doing to society.  Think about this.<P>I want to work on a car.  I spend a couple of minutes at home and all of a sudden detailed instructions on how to do this arcane activity, replacing the roof of a car, are available.  From all over the world!   <P>Folks, this is the greatest thing since the invention of movable type.  We live in a remarkable time.    <p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 10-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||10-11-2000||04:26 PM||||Hey, hey wait now.. we're not finished yet   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>After you're got the new roof in, go to a automotive glass install place and see if you can get a new gasket strip for around the windshield to replace the old one.  I used the wider 3/8" to 1/2" strip so the corners don't pull in like the stock thin seals do when they get old.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Xtreme Fiero's||10-11-2000||06:14 PM||||I have a factory sunroof could this be done the make it a hard top? I just like the hard top better.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||10-11-2000||06:34 PM||||All Fieros have the hole in the frame for the sun roof the only difference is the plastic exterior top and the headliner. In fact if you put everyting together with velcro you could swap back for summer and winter.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ed||10-11-2000||07:16 PM||||X-Fiero:<P>Want to trade?  Not really! <P>Why get rid of the sunroof? If it leaks, it can be fixed.  If its too bright, get the sunshade for it.  See archives for details.<P>Anyway, I meant what I said about the internet. <P>Others: Thanks for the info.  I'm about to negotiate for a "Ferrino" with a 160 hp Quad Four, with five-speed. (No sunroof)  This is one of the Ferrari 308 knock-offs.  I've asked around, and its apparently one of the better replicars.  Engine was professionally installed by Huffaker, who prepared GTU Fieros (See discussion in Witzenburg's book, and threads on what a GTU car was).||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tigger||10-11-2000||11:53 PM||||Hey, I think we're all glad we maybe helped out Ed.  You're welcome.||||reg||1
